00:12:13.721 --> 00:12:14.932
So it's going
to be interesting to see


00:12:14.956 --> 00:12:16.766
how when we're
all done with this,


00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:20.371
exactly how many people's lives
have been affected.


00:12:20.395 --> 00:12:21.972
And, really, you have to factor
in their families


00:12:21.996 --> 00:12:23.708
and everyone they
had contact with and,


00:12:23.732 --> 00:12:25.308
you know, since the time,


00:12:25.333 --> 00:12:27.111
'cause that's gonna be
impossible to track,


00:12:27.135 --> 00:12:30.015
but, you know, it's just...
It's just a lot of people,


00:12:30.039 --> 00:12:32.098
many more than I think
people realize.


00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.925
From the very beginning,
I felt this way.


00:12:40.949 --> 00:12:42.827
We had to bring justice
to these victims,


00:12:42.851 --> 00:12:45.162
and even the cases beyond
the statute of limitations.


00:12:45.187 --> 00:12:48.701
I knew I wanted
to get them all tested.


00:12:48.725 --> 00:12:50.954
DNA testing can identify
an unknown perpetrator.


00:12:52.861 --> 00:12:56.909
It can confirm the presence
of a known suspect.


00:12:56.933 --> 00:12:59.732
It can affirm a survivor's
account of their assault.


00:13:01.137 --> 00:13:03.682
It can connect the suspect
to other crime scenes,


00:13:03.706 --> 00:13:05.732
even those in other states
and jurisdictions.


00:13:07.110 --> 00:13:08.753
It can help solve other cases


00:13:08.777 --> 00:13:11.122
that may not even be
sexual assaults.


00:13:11.146 --> 00:13:12.858
It can also,
and this is very important,


00:13:12.882 --> 00:13:15.327
exonerate the innocent.


00:13:15.351 --> 00:13:18.343
Bottom line,
a rape kit can bring justice.


00:13:23.126 --> 00:13:26.839
So all of these people meet for the
first and third Thursday of every month


00:13:26.863 --> 00:13:28.374
for at least two hours


00:13:28.398 --> 00:13:33.712
to talk about
how we got into this mess.


00:13:33.736 --> 00:13:38.017
On DPD's end, I was tasked
with making sure that


00:13:38.042 --> 00:13:42.155
we provided what was needed
to make this project work.


00:13:42.179 --> 00:13:45.391
And, you know, there was several
things that was dropped in my lap


00:13:45.416 --> 00:13:49.130
that I didn't know how I was
going to be able to get through it.


00:13:49.154 --> 00:13:53.900
Or looking at the officers that,
you know, investigate these cases,


00:13:53.924 --> 00:13:56.904
they were overwhelmed,
stressed out.


00:13:56.928 --> 00:13:58.759
How will we bring this
all together?


00:14:00.665 --> 00:14:02.643
My job in this project was to


00:14:02.667 --> 00:14:04.678
sort of watch this group
come together,


00:14:04.703 --> 00:14:08.583
to document all of the decisions
they had to make.


00:14:08.607 --> 00:14:09.984
You know, when you find out
that you have 11,000 kits,


00:14:10.008 --> 00:14:10.985
what do you do?


00:14:11.009 --> 00:14:14.022
It happened over decades.


00:14:14.046 --> 00:14:16.724
What was
the decision-making process


00:14:16.748 --> 00:14:18.860
that said,
with limited resources,


00:14:18.885 --> 00:14:23.965
I'm choosing not to send
this kit, but I'll send this kit?


00:14:23.989 --> 00:14:27.903
But the decision not to send
that kit was far more common


00:14:27.927 --> 00:14:29.805
than the decision
to send the kit.


00:14:31.965 --> 00:14:36.177
Sol started piecing this together
in the late '90s, early 2000s.


00:14:36.202 --> 00:14:38.715
And then we had
New York City break,


00:14:38.739 --> 00:14:40.682
and we had Los Angeles break,


00:14:40.706 --> 00:14:42.384
and then the community
I've worked with, Detroit,


00:14:42.408 --> 00:14:44.020
where it showed up in the media.


00:14:44.044 --> 00:14:46.122
And there was
this huge sort of surprise,


00:14:46.146 --> 00:14:48.057
but for those of us who have
been in this movement for a while,


00:14:48.081 --> 00:14:50.792
it's like,
"This wasn't a surprise.


00:14:50.816 --> 00:14:54.047
They have not been testing
these kits for decades."


00:14:55.856 --> 00:14:59.168
In Detroit, certainly
finances were an issue.


00:14:59.192 --> 00:15:02.639
It costs money to test a kit.


00:15:02.663 --> 00:15:07.076
The money for this in Detroit
was just not there.


00:15:07.100 --> 00:15:10.613
But why didn't they take
the money they did have


00:15:10.637 --> 00:15:12.581
and put it in this crime?


00:15:12.606 --> 00:15:14.717
That's when you have to dig
into this a little bit deeper.


00:15:14.741 --> 00:15:16.352
You have to open up
the police reports


00:15:16.376 --> 00:15:18.854
associated with the kits
that were not tested.


00:15:18.879 --> 00:15:20.858
And when you do that,
that's when you


00:15:20.882 --> 00:15:23.627
start getting a much
more complicated story.


00:15:30.058 --> 00:15:32.903
Detroit is
primarily African-American,


00:15:32.927 --> 00:15:34.771
so over 80% of them


00:15:34.796 --> 00:15:38.076
were from African-American
residents of the city.


00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:40.744
By our best estimates,
most of them were poor,


00:15:40.768 --> 00:15:44.069
so we're talking
about poor black women.


00:15:46.075 --> 00:15:48.873
It's pretty clear they did
not believe the victims.


00:15:53.882 --> 00:15:55.894
They fundamentally
did not believe


00:15:55.919 --> 00:15:58.063
what happened
to them was a crime.


00:15:58.087 --> 00:16:01.216
They did not believe that what
happened to them merited their attention.


00:16:12.102 --> 00:16:14.847
We read police reports
where victims


00:16:14.871 --> 00:16:20.208
were called bitches,
ho's, whores, heifers.


00:16:23.013 --> 00:16:25.691
They said, "Eh, it's not
really a real sexual assault.


00:16:25.715 --> 00:16:28.128
This is not worth
our investigative time,


00:16:28.152 --> 00:16:32.179
and it's certainly not worth our
limited resources of testing this kit."


00:16:35.191 --> 00:16:36.802
I carried the pager.


00:16:36.826 --> 00:16:38.071
I was one
of those social workers


00:16:38.095 --> 00:16:39.872
that responded
to sexual assaults


00:16:39.896 --> 00:16:41.774
when they happened
in the city of Detroit.


00:16:41.798 --> 00:16:46.212
And if I get a phone call
from a nurse's aide who says,


00:16:46.237 --> 00:16:47.881
"Girl, if you're
in the middle of your lunch,


00:16:47.905 --> 00:16:50.115
get down here when you can,"


00:16:50.141 --> 00:16:53.120
I knew that I was either gonna
interface with a woman of color,


00:16:53.144 --> 00:16:56.291
or a woman who might have
had a substance abuse issue,


00:16:56.315 --> 00:16:59.793
and didn't belong to anybody,
if you know what I mean.


00:16:59.817 --> 00:17:02.095
But if I got a call
from the doctor,


00:17:02.119 --> 00:17:04.865
and they would say,
"Hurry up and get down here,"


00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:06.800
it was one of two kind of women.


00:17:06.824 --> 00:17:08.869
It was either a white woman


00:17:08.894 --> 00:17:12.774
or a black politically connected
woman in the city of Detroit.


00:17:12.798 --> 00:17:15.977
Either she was a pastor's
daughter or a politician's daughter


00:17:16.001 --> 00:17:18.079
or something like that.


00:17:18.103 --> 00:17:20.682
And it was a pattern,
and I'm not saying that...


00:17:20.707 --> 00:17:23.971
I'm not saying it happened all
the time, but it was noticeable.


00:17:25.777 --> 00:17:27.722
<i>I believe that there's just a...</i>


00:17:27.747 --> 00:17:32.928
a lesser sense of value
for women who are black,


00:17:32.952 --> 00:17:34.196
that they're just not...
Not human enough


00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:35.931
to even be rapeable.


00:17:35.955 --> 00:17:38.768
And so these cases
just got thrown to the side


00:17:38.792 --> 00:17:41.089
and not thought about
in any particular way.


00:17:43.162 --> 00:17:45.741
With the darker
pigment of your skin,


00:17:45.766 --> 00:17:47.943
your life seems
to have less value


00:17:47.967 --> 00:17:49.811
in the criminal justice system.
That's true.


00:17:49.836 --> 00:17:52.048
You're just taken more
seriously in this country,


00:17:52.072 --> 00:17:55.384
in all aspects, if you have
money or if you have influence


00:17:55.409 --> 00:17:58.003
or if you have power
or if you have privilege.


00:18:03.251 --> 00:18:05.295
- It's a national problem.
- Sure, that's right.


00:18:05.319 --> 00:18:08.998
There's over 400,000 backlog
untested kits in this country.


00:18:09.022 --> 00:18:10.901
Talk to me, though,
about what that means,


00:18:10.925 --> 00:18:12.136
the... the... the testing
of these kits.


00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:14.736
How you get from testing it
to saying,


00:18:14.761 --> 00:18:17.307
"Well, here are the people
that we need to start looking at."


00:18:17.331 --> 00:18:20.744
The scientists will take
whatever evidence is in the kit,


00:18:20.768 --> 00:18:22.078
the saliva, hair, fibers,


00:18:22.102 --> 00:18:25.147
anything to help get
a genetic profile


00:18:25.172 --> 00:18:27.217
that can then be loaded
and entered into CODIS.


00:18:27.242 --> 00:18:29.954
And CODIS, C-O-D-I-S,
is the DNA national database.


00:18:29.978 --> 00:18:31.221
Right.


00:18:31.245 --> 00:18:33.056
But there are
two different kind of hits.


00:18:33.080 --> 00:18:34.158
It either matches
to a person's profile


00:18:34.182 --> 00:18:35.860
already in CODIS,


00:18:35.884 --> 00:18:37.828
or it can match
to another case through DNA


00:18:37.852 --> 00:18:39.362
from evidence, such as clothing,


00:18:39.387 --> 00:18:41.131
even if that person's
identity is unknown.


00:18:41.156 --> 00:18:42.817
Yeah.


00:18:44.859 --> 00:18:47.871
So now the next critical
step is the investigation


00:18:47.896 --> 00:18:49.306
and prosecution of these kits.


00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.110
Right, and so that next step
is saying,


00:18:52.134 --> 00:18:54.846
"All right, well,
let's go find these people


00:18:54.870 --> 00:18:56.346
and bring them to justice."


00:18:56.371 --> 00:18:58.617
"Let's take each individual
hit, work up a case


00:18:58.641 --> 00:19:00.786
the way it should have been
worked up when it first happened."


00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:01.887
So you have to still go
find the victim,


00:19:01.911 --> 00:19:03.054
you have to find the defendant,


00:19:03.078 --> 00:19:04.789
you have to find the witnesses,


00:19:04.814 --> 00:19:06.925
any other evidence besides
just the DNA evidence.


00:19:06.949 --> 00:19:08.960
And put the case together
the old-fashioned way.


00:19:08.984 --> 00:19:11.263
And that... that gets
to all the problems


00:19:11.288 --> 00:19:13.165
- that you have with resources.
- Exactly.


00:19:13.189 --> 00:19:14.900
You just don't have
enough people.


00:19:14.924 --> 00:19:16.235
Exactly. Up until...


00:19:16.259 --> 00:19:18.204
Up until a couple
of months ago, we had two.


00:19:18.228 --> 00:19:20.806
And contrast that
with the city of Cleveland,


00:19:20.831 --> 00:19:23.276
who discovered about
4,000 kits several years ago.


00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.278
They had 35 investigators.


00:19:25.302 --> 00:19:27.013
I think 25 or 35 investigators,


00:19:27.037 --> 00:19:28.983
- to our two that we had.
- To two.


00:19:52.264 --> 00:19:55.642
In 2009, a guy named
Anthony Sowell was caught,


00:19:55.666 --> 00:19:58.912
and he was a serial rapist
and murderer.


00:20:02.406 --> 00:20:04.884
And at some point, several women


00:20:04.909 --> 00:20:06.687
reported
that he had attacked them.


00:20:06.711 --> 00:20:08.655
And there were questions
whether some of those reports


00:20:08.679 --> 00:20:10.204
were taken seriously or not.


00:20:15.720 --> 00:20:18.933
It ended up when the police
were investigating a report


00:20:18.957 --> 00:20:21.935
in late October of 2009,


00:20:21.959 --> 00:20:24.171
they went to the house
to look for him,


00:20:24.196 --> 00:20:26.636
and in the house, they discovered
the bodies of several women.


00:20:30.335 --> 00:20:36.002
In total, it was 11 women who
Anthony Sowell raped and murdered.


00:20:40.178 --> 00:20:42.691
After that case, our editors
at the "Plain Dealer"


00:20:42.715 --> 00:20:46.161
asked us to really dig in
to how sex crimes were handled.


00:20:46.185 --> 00:20:49.665
And a part of that was
the question of rape kits.


00:20:49.689 --> 00:20:52.000
We knew that
there were a number of them


00:20:52.025 --> 00:20:55.437
related to the Sowell case
that had not been tested.


00:20:55.461 --> 00:20:58.306
That part for us was really
difficult to see how many people


00:20:58.331 --> 00:21:03.913
had been attacked long after the
evidence was there and hadn't been used.


00:21:03.937 --> 00:21:06.349
So we wanted to ask
the larger question.


00:21:06.373 --> 00:21:08.184
You know, how many
of these rape kits


00:21:08.208 --> 00:21:10.954
here in Cleveland
haven't been tested?


00:21:10.978 --> 00:21:13.623
When they were done counting
and sorting through the kits,


00:21:13.647 --> 00:21:15.992
they had a little over 4,000


00:21:16.016 --> 00:21:18.247
that previously
had not been tested.


00:21:26.194 --> 00:21:28.305
Of course we made mistakes


00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:31.275
as a law enforcement community.


00:21:31.299 --> 00:21:33.343
We didn't realize the potential


00:21:33.368 --> 00:21:36.248
of what sat behind us
in the locker.


00:21:36.272 --> 00:21:40.784
In this building were these
untested rape kits gathering dust.


00:21:40.808 --> 00:21:42.106
We did not appreciate it.


00:21:44.179 --> 00:21:47.390
I was shocked
by how many of these


00:21:47.414 --> 00:21:49.793
we got hits or matches on


00:21:49.818 --> 00:21:52.296
when we took this unknown DNA,


00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:54.331
had no idea who it was,


00:21:54.356 --> 00:21:56.334
put it
in the national DNA database


00:21:56.358 --> 00:22:01.073
and Ohio's database
with known profiles,


00:22:01.097 --> 00:22:02.341
and we got a hit.


00:22:11.272 --> 00:22:14.052
Those kits start getting tested,
they start getting results,


00:22:14.076 --> 00:22:16.221
and then you have almost
another type of backlog,


00:22:16.245 --> 00:22:17.789
an investigative backlog.


00:22:17.813 --> 00:22:20.225
Hundreds of new cases
were coming in.


00:22:20.249 --> 00:22:22.727
How do you figure out
what to do with them?


00:22:22.752 --> 00:22:25.632
How do you figure out which is the
most important one to handle first?


00:22:29.425 --> 00:22:31.738
He's got those three events,


00:22:31.762 --> 00:22:34.173
all related close in time.


00:22:34.197 --> 00:22:37.042
The DNA evidence that
was able to be collected


00:22:37.067 --> 00:22:40.413
from the clothing, from the the
pockets, and off the sports bra


00:22:40.437 --> 00:22:43.066
of victim number one
and victim number two.


00:22:44.809 --> 00:22:46.319
And as the cases were coming in,


00:22:46.343 --> 00:22:47.720
they weren't
really triaging them


00:22:47.744 --> 00:22:49.723
to see who was in prison,


00:22:49.747 --> 00:22:51.358
who was out on the streets.


00:22:51.382 --> 00:22:54.361
And so they got a hit
for a 2000 rape


00:22:54.385 --> 00:22:59.733
in which the potential suspect
was James W. Daniel Ill.


00:22:59.757 --> 00:23:02.302
And several months went by,


00:23:02.326 --> 00:23:05.172
and in that time
before he was arrested,


00:23:05.196 --> 00:23:07.097
he raped two other women.


00:23:09.034 --> 00:23:10.344
And they had
a surveillance video


00:23:10.369 --> 00:23:12.132
that showed part of this attack.


00:23:43.703 --> 00:23:46.214
This case really
knocked some of the folks


00:23:46.238 --> 00:23:48.115
on the task force
back a little bit,


00:23:48.140 --> 00:23:50.686
because they were so proud
of what they were doing,


00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:54.755
but at the same time, they realized
that they had to prioritize the cases.


00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.793
And you can see
just how much harm it is


00:23:57.817 --> 00:24:00.262
for not having tackled
this in the past.


00:24:00.286 --> 00:24:02.618
- We're not keeping up.
- No, we're not keeping up.


00:24:04.791 --> 00:24:07.804
People who they became
aware of as a suspect,


00:24:07.828 --> 00:24:09.739
they had to ask themselves
the question.


00:24:09.764 --> 00:24:11.107
Are they on
the street right now?


00:24:11.131 --> 00:24:13.075
Could they attack somebody else?


00:24:13.099 --> 00:24:15.278
We're still working
on prioritizing the cases


00:24:15.302 --> 00:24:17.647
that are rolling in.


00:24:17.671 --> 00:24:22.385
I know that Nicole has a guy
with two cases out on the street.


00:24:34.189 --> 00:24:36.199
Each of the investigators


00:24:36.223 --> 00:24:38.136
has anywhere from 80 to
90 cases on their docket.


00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:41.773
And every day,
we get another 20 to 30 hits.


00:24:41.797 --> 00:24:45.843
If there's a statue
of limitations about to run,


00:24:45.867 --> 00:24:50.849
or if we have a serial offender
who's out on the streets,


00:24:50.873 --> 00:24:52.806
those go to the top
of our priority list.


00:24:54.442 --> 00:24:57.155
Okay, we have a hit to a suspect


00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.392
who has been identified
in three cases,


00:25:00.416 --> 00:25:03.695
one in '95, one in 1998.


00:25:03.719 --> 00:25:08.333
And this third victim that
we're trying to locate and notify


00:25:08.357 --> 00:25:11.335
is a hit to a 1997 case.


00:25:11.359 --> 00:25:16.675
So we're going to go attempt to
find her at her last known address,


00:25:16.699 --> 00:25:21.136
and let her know that we have
new information on her case.


00:25:32.683 --> 00:25:33.683
Danielle?


00:25:36.385 --> 00:25:39.231
So the reason we're here


00:25:39.256 --> 00:25:44.304
is because of the rape
in January of '97.


00:25:44.328 --> 00:25:48.908
What can you tell me
about what you remember?


00:25:48.932 --> 00:25:51.411
I lived next door to, like,
a little convenience store.


00:25:51.436 --> 00:25:53.179
Okay.


00:25:53.203 --> 00:25:58.285
And, um, I met a guy
that went to that store.


00:25:58.309 --> 00:26:02.154
I think he asked me if I wanted
to smoke a joint or something.


00:26:02.179 --> 00:26:05.793
We went to, I guess, one of
his friend's houses or something.


00:26:05.817 --> 00:26:09.231
He grabbed me by my neck,
and he pushed me down.


00:26:09.254 --> 00:26:12.666
And I started screaming


00:26:12.690 --> 00:26:15.870
for somebody to help me.


00:26:15.895 --> 00:26:18.673
And I remember, 'cause
there were people upstairs...


00:26:20.231 --> 00:26:21.743
and nobody came down.


00:26:32.278 --> 00:26:34.357
<i>He, um...</i>


00:26:34.381 --> 00:26:37.859
He started pulling my pants
down, and he grabbed my arms,


00:26:37.883 --> 00:26:39.995
and he held them real tight.


00:26:40.020 --> 00:26:43.331
And he said, "If you don't shut the
fuck up, I'm gonna beat your ass."


00:26:43.355 --> 00:26:46.235
And so I shut up.


00:26:46.259 --> 00:26:48.970
And he finished,


00:26:48.994 --> 00:26:50.305
and I got up,
and I sat in a chair,


00:26:50.329 --> 00:26:52.740
and I just cried and cried.


00:26:52.765 --> 00:26:54.324
And I didn't
really know what to do.


00:26:55.868 --> 00:26:58.781
And then I started feeling,


00:26:58.805 --> 00:27:00.882
I don't know,
like it was my fault.


00:27:00.906 --> 00:27:03.252
And that I shouldn't have
put myself in that position.


00:27:03.276 --> 00:27:04.920
You know it's
not your fault now, right?


00:27:04.945 --> 00:27:07.189
Yeah, I know now.


00:27:07.213 --> 00:27:09.291
All right, the series of photos here


00:27:09.316 --> 00:27:12.194
may or may not contain him
in the photographs.


00:27:12.218 --> 00:27:15.712
And try not to pay attention
to anything other than his face.


00:27:20.360 --> 00:27:21.638
That one.


00:27:21.662 --> 00:27:23.373
How sure are you?


00:27:23.397 --> 00:27:25.608
- I'm 100% sure it's him.
- 100%?


00:27:25.633 --> 00:27:27.176
- Yeah.
- Okay.


00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:28.645
Oh, my God.


00:27:28.669 --> 00:27:30.213
If you could just circle him.


00:27:30.237 --> 00:27:32.604
- Sorry.
- That's okay.


00:27:38.412 --> 00:27:40.848
And I have to let you
know he's not in custody yet.


00:27:42.984 --> 00:27:46.297
We are going to try and get him
off the streets as soon as possible.


00:27:46.321 --> 00:27:47.865
So there's
two of us right now, right?


00:27:47.889 --> 00:27:49.867
Two local.
There's a third hit.


00:27:49.891 --> 00:27:54.886
So there are three different
DNA hits to this suspect.


00:28:04.707 --> 00:28:07.586
I think for us in Cleveland,


00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.820
one of the most striking things
that we learned


00:28:09.845 --> 00:28:13.359
was that far more of
the suspects in these cases


00:28:13.383 --> 00:28:14.994
than we ever would have thought


00:28:15.018 --> 00:28:16.929
were suspected serial rapists.


00:28:16.953 --> 00:28:19.832
We did not expect to find


00:28:19.856 --> 00:28:22.035
that a third or more
of the people


00:28:22.059 --> 00:28:23.686
were linked to multiple cases.


00:28:25.895 --> 00:28:27.973
Most men are not rapists,


00:28:27.997 --> 00:28:32.978
but the ones who are perpetrate
again and again and again.


00:28:56.393 --> 00:29:00.673
About four days
after my 17th birthday,


00:29:00.697 --> 00:29:02.241
it was in 1996,


00:29:02.266 --> 00:29:03.843
I had been given
a Volkswagen Rabbit,


00:29:03.867 --> 00:29:05.813
a HEW car.


00:29:05.837 --> 00:29:07.847
And I was obsessed with the car.


00:29:07.871 --> 00:29:09.615
I washed it constantly.


00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:12.654
And I had taken it
to a local car wash.


00:29:16.081 --> 00:29:18.675
So I was there, and it was dark.


00:29:21.786 --> 00:29:24.032
And as I was washing it,
a man approached me


00:29:24.056 --> 00:29:27.650
with a rag over his face,
asking for help.


00:29:29.762 --> 00:29:32.340
That man abducted me
at knife point


00:29:32.364 --> 00:29:35.009
and had me captive
for about ten hours,


00:29:35.034 --> 00:29:37.680
during which time I was
repeatedly assaulted.


00:29:43.409 --> 00:29:46.721
He took $20 from my wallet.


00:29:46.745 --> 00:29:49.391
He took the money,
and he took my license.


00:29:49.416 --> 00:29:53.396
He took the license, he said, so
that he would know where I lived.


00:29:53.420 --> 00:29:56.799
And he was very clear
that if I reported,


00:29:56.823 --> 00:30:01.170
he would come back and
kill me and kill my family.


00:30:03.997 --> 00:30:06.143
He eventually did release me,


00:30:06.167 --> 00:30:08.711
and then I went,


00:30:08.735 --> 00:30:10.012
and I flagged a police cruiser,


00:30:10.036 --> 00:30:12.881
the first cruiser that I saw.


00:30:12.906 --> 00:30:14.917
I knew that I had to report.


00:30:14.941 --> 00:30:17.953
I knew I couldn't go home,


00:30:17.978 --> 00:30:19.922
and even though
I was in a state of shock,


00:30:19.946 --> 00:30:22.057
I knew that I had to do
something.


00:30:26.786 --> 00:30:29.899
Eventually I was taken
to an area hospital


00:30:29.924 --> 00:30:31.949
where the rape kit
was performed.


00:30:34.728 --> 00:30:36.009
The process takes several hours.


00:30:40.301 --> 00:30:43.713
Open up your legs
like a butterfly, okay?


00:30:43.738 --> 00:30:46.717
I'm gonna pull
a few pubic hairs.


00:30:46.741 --> 00:30:48.820
And a humiliating process,


00:30:48.844 --> 00:30:50.954
not only because
of the physical invasion,


00:30:50.978 --> 00:30:55.059
but because of the kind of questioning
that was going on at the time.


00:30:55.083 --> 00:30:58.062
All of the questions that
I got from law enforcement


00:30:58.086 --> 00:31:03.934
were asked with the intention of finding
out what I did to cause the assault.


00:31:03.959 --> 00:31:05.703
What was
I doing at the car wash?


00:31:05.727 --> 00:31:07.738
Did I know the person
who assaulted me?


00:31:07.763 --> 00:31:11.825
I felt blamed,
I felt blamed right away.


00:31:14.904 --> 00:31:16.814
My parents are both immigrants.


00:31:16.838 --> 00:31:20.118
My mother is from Cuba, and
my father is from Argentina.


00:31:20.143 --> 00:31:22.421
And when the assault took place,


00:31:22.445 --> 00:31:28.060
they had a lot of held
mistrust for law enforcement,


00:31:28.084 --> 00:31:31.988
and an expectation
that we wouldn't be helped.


00:31:33.756 --> 00:31:37.737
And they were right
to think that.


00:31:37.761 --> 00:31:39.172
That's the American way,


00:31:39.196 --> 00:31:42.976
this inequality within
the criminal justice system


00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:44.877
when it comes
to people of color,


00:31:44.902 --> 00:31:48.950
or the economic classes
and the difference.


00:31:48.974 --> 00:31:52.986
So that is so endemic
to our society,


00:31:53.010 --> 00:31:55.321
of course it's
going to have an impact,


00:31:55.346 --> 00:31:57.939
you know, with police
and police investigation.


00:31:59.749 --> 00:32:01.061
When we first started,


00:32:01.085 --> 00:32:03.897
one of the biggest problems
was the police,


00:32:03.921 --> 00:32:06.866
and a lot of other programs
were discouraging victims


00:32:06.890 --> 00:32:08.768
from making police reports,


00:32:08.792 --> 00:32:10.770
because they felt they would be
mistreated by the police,


00:32:10.794 --> 00:32:12.786
and in many cases they were.


00:32:17.835 --> 00:32:21.082
How many rape kits
do you have at Central Storage?


00:32:21.106 --> 00:32:23.817
Off the top of our heads,
we could not tell you right now.


00:32:23.841 --> 00:32:26.153
When the Sheriff's
Department finally counted,


00:32:26.177 --> 00:32:29.156
they found nearly 5,000 kits
in storage.


00:32:29.180 --> 00:32:33.060
300 of them were past
the statute of limitations.


00:32:33.084 --> 00:32:34.929
And it was very
confusing in the beginning.


00:32:34.953 --> 00:32:36.898
We heard that there
were 7,000 kits,


00:32:36.922 --> 00:32:39.734
and there were 11,000 kits,
and there were 12,000 kits.


00:32:39.759 --> 00:32:42.971
It was fascinating in terms
of the confusion


00:32:42.995 --> 00:32:45.373
around
what was really happening.


00:32:45.398 --> 00:32:48.377
And then we found out
that kits had been destroyed,


00:32:48.401 --> 00:32:49.994
kits had been thrown away.


00:32:52.104 --> 00:32:53.849
In terms of the destruction of evidence,


00:32:53.873 --> 00:32:55.952
I think that may have occurred


00:32:55.976 --> 00:32:58.887
in the L.A. County
Sheriff's Department.


00:32:58.911 --> 00:33:01.824
They had, in our view,


00:33:01.848 --> 00:33:03.959
misinterpreted
the statute of limitations


00:33:03.984 --> 00:33:06.161
and had been
destroying rape kits,


00:33:06.185 --> 00:33:08.883
thinking the statute had run
when it hadn't.


00:33:10.324 --> 00:33:15.204
Therefore, there were rapists
who were not caught.


00:33:15.228 --> 00:33:20.310
And there were survivors
who lived in fear


00:33:20.335 --> 00:33:22.979
of maybe the rapist
was going to come back.


00:33:36.317 --> 00:33:38.361
When something
like this has happened


00:33:38.386 --> 00:33:40.786
and you don't know
where that person is...


00:33:43.991 --> 00:33:45.191
that means they're everywhere.


00:33:47.929 --> 00:33:50.808
They are over your shoulder.


00:33:50.832 --> 00:33:54.912
They're gonna be inside the
front door when you walk in.


00:33:54.936 --> 00:33:58.849
I would come home at night
and open the door


00:33:58.873 --> 00:34:01.852
and imagine his hands
coming up to my throat.


00:34:01.877 --> 00:34:03.988
I could feel his hands
coming up to my throat.


00:34:04.012 --> 00:34:06.723
Going up the stairs
to my room, in my room,


00:34:06.748 --> 00:34:10.195
every sound you hear is them
breaking into the house.


00:34:10.219 --> 00:34:12.297
I would have
terrible nightmares.


00:34:12.321 --> 00:34:15.300
I would wake up each morning
and imagine going downstairs


00:34:15.324 --> 00:34:17.869
and finding my family dead.


00:34:17.893 --> 00:34:19.337
I was afraid to leave my house.


00:34:19.361 --> 00:34:23.841
There was a time during
which I was not functioning.


00:34:23.866 --> 00:34:25.800
I was not a functioning person.


00:34:31.874 --> 00:34:33.969
And I can't understand...


00:34:35.378 --> 00:34:37.156
why I was so unimportant,


00:34:37.180 --> 00:34:39.825
what was
so unimportant about me,


00:34:39.849 --> 00:34:42.427
that someone couldn't just
take a little bit of their time


00:34:42.452 --> 00:34:44.011
and help me find out.


00:35:07.111 --> 00:35:09.757
I do want to take
the time just to say thank you


00:35:09.781 --> 00:35:11.959
for, you know, the things
that you've poured into me


00:35:11.983 --> 00:35:13.793
and into my girls.


00:35:13.818 --> 00:35:15.763
You know, being
a single mom is not easy.


00:35:15.787 --> 00:35:18.165
It's kinda like when you're
going through your journey,


00:35:18.190 --> 00:35:20.400
and you don't have resources


00:35:20.424 --> 00:35:21.968
or you don't have money
for resources.


00:35:21.992 --> 00:35:23.036
Like, "Well, how do I cope?"


00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.938
Sometimes I'm kinda scared,


00:35:24.962 --> 00:35:27.074
because, you know, as a mom
you don't want your kids


00:35:27.098 --> 00:35:30.077
to see you as anything flawed
or anything like that.


00:35:30.101 --> 00:35:32.078
And in the
African-American community,


00:35:32.102 --> 00:35:34.048
you know, we have other myths.


00:35:34.072 --> 00:35:36.417
You know, where you can't tell
people what happened to you.


00:35:36.441 --> 00:35:37.985
You don't have faith in God.


00:35:38.009 --> 00:35:39.253
- Just go to God.
- Just go to God with it


00:35:39.277 --> 00:35:41.255
and let it be there.


00:35:41.279 --> 00:35:43.657
I mean, this can go back to
slavery how we were forced to,


00:35:43.682 --> 00:35:46.261
you know,
carry things in silence.


00:35:46.285 --> 00:35:49.263
You know, our pain, our
anguish, all type of stuff.


00:35:49.287 --> 00:35:51.666
- I say speak up.
- Right.


00:35:51.691 --> 00:35:53.202
If you are going
through something


00:35:53.226 --> 00:35:54.436
you don't know
how to get out of it,


00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:58.206
don't hold anything in.


00:36:01.334 --> 00:36:05.180
After my incident,


00:36:05.204 --> 00:36:06.933
I was very depressed.


00:36:09.308 --> 00:36:10.970
And I didn't have a reason.


00:36:12.246 --> 00:36:13.872
I didn't have a reason to live.


00:36:16.183 --> 00:36:21.396
The insecurities
and all of the different fears.


00:36:21.420 --> 00:36:23.185
Now it's just an open wound.


00:36:25.392 --> 00:36:30.193
I do feel like a big piece
of me was taken away from me.


00:36:33.133 --> 00:36:35.102
You know,
I'll never be the same.


00:36:38.273 --> 00:36:41.285
I will be 32 this year.


00:36:41.309 --> 00:36:45.122
That was my 21 st birthday,
so 11 years.


00:36:46.948 --> 00:36:48.279
No.


00:36:51.085 --> 00:36:53.031
I... I wasn't waiting.
I stop...


00:36:53.055 --> 00:36:56.700
I gave it up, on that day.


00:36:56.724 --> 00:37:00.171
I've never waited,
because I couldn't wait.


00:37:00.195 --> 00:37:01.372
I couldn't think about it.


00:37:01.397 --> 00:37:04.909
I couldn't even expect anything.


00:37:04.933 --> 00:37:06.310
Or I would not, I would not,


00:37:06.334 --> 00:37:07.927
I don't think
I would be here today.


00:37:24.687 --> 00:37:26.264
For so many years


00:37:26.288 --> 00:37:28.334
after the police dropped
my case essentially,


00:37:28.358 --> 00:37:30.268
every once in awhile,
I would see something


00:37:30.292 --> 00:37:33.071
or experience something
that triggered the memory.


00:37:33.095 --> 00:37:38.710
And I wondered, "Is it possible
that my kit is still out there?"


00:37:38.734 --> 00:37:40.679
I had to keep going
higher and higher and higher


00:37:40.704 --> 00:37:43.749
in the level of people I was
getting to advocate for me,


00:37:43.773 --> 00:37:46.986
to a point where I had,
you know, an ex-D.A.


00:37:47.010 --> 00:37:49.021
calling the Sheriff's Department


00:37:49.045 --> 00:37:51.757
to ask, "What happened
with Helena's case?


00:37:51.781 --> 00:37:53.393
You need to tell us more."


00:37:53.417 --> 00:37:57.396
And within two weeks,
I had a call.


00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:59.014
<i>I had a call...</i>


00:38:01.626 --> 00:38:04.136
from the Sheriff's Department,


00:38:04.160 --> 00:38:07.062
who hadn't called me back
for 14 years.


00:38:11.969 --> 00:38:15.382
And they came to my house
with what they call the six-pack.


00:38:15.406 --> 00:38:18.920
And even though it had been
more than a decade,


00:38:18.944 --> 00:38:22.957
I knew right away
which one he was.


00:38:22.981 --> 00:38:25.726
<i>And it was eventually
revealed to me</i>


00:38:25.751 --> 00:38:27.962
that the kit had
actually been processed


00:38:27.986 --> 00:38:32.700
three or four years prior to my
advocates becoming involved,


00:38:32.724 --> 00:38:37.305
that it had matched to
Charles Courtney in that time,


00:38:37.329 --> 00:38:40.041
and that no one
had followed up on it.


00:38:40.065 --> 00:38:42.144
I feel like a fool
when someone says,


00:38:42.168 --> 00:38:44.213
"Well, why didn't they process?
Why didn't they call you?


00:38:44.237 --> 00:38:46.279
They had a match.
What happened?"


00:38:46.303 --> 00:38:48.215
I don't know,
and I can't tell you,


00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:50.118
because they wouldn't tell me,


00:38:50.142 --> 00:38:51.974
other than
it fell through the cracks.


00:38:54.780 --> 00:38:58.326
Because the DNA from my kit had
not been processed, he went home.


00:38:58.350 --> 00:39:00.962
He was... He was
a long-distance truck driver,


00:39:00.987 --> 00:39:03.031
which is
another very troubling fact.


00:39:03.055 --> 00:39:04.632
The fact that
he had access to victims


00:39:04.656 --> 00:39:06.935
between at least
Los Angeles and Ohio.


00:39:06.959 --> 00:39:11.006
There could be who knows
how many other victims.


00:39:29.115 --> 00:39:31.084
You getting out your piano?


00:39:35.289 --> 00:39:37.267
It's playing a song.


00:39:44.065 --> 00:39:48.178
It happened April 21st, 1998.


00:39:48.203 --> 00:39:52.049
I dropped off some film at the
grocery store to get developed,


00:39:52.073 --> 00:39:55.052
and I wanted to stop by there
after I got off work


00:39:55.076 --> 00:39:59.056
on my way home that night
to pick it up.


00:39:59.080 --> 00:40:01.793
He was out in the parking lot,
lingering around.


00:40:01.817 --> 00:40:04.728
Really didn't pay
much attention.


00:40:04.753 --> 00:40:08.366
And I went in and got my film,
wasn't in there that long.


00:40:08.390 --> 00:40:12.237
Came back out, and he was
still out in the parking lot.


00:40:12.261 --> 00:40:15.206
Still didn't register in my head


00:40:15.231 --> 00:40:18.444
anything bad
was going to happen.


00:40:18.468 --> 00:40:21.312
And, um, I went
to go get in my car,


00:40:21.337 --> 00:40:25.718
and he came up behind me
and threatened me.


00:40:25.742 --> 00:40:27.369
Said he had a weapon.


00:40:30.145 --> 00:40:33.458
And there was a parking lot
that had a bunch of trailers,


00:40:33.482 --> 00:40:35.314
truck trailers sitting in them.


00:40:37.453 --> 00:40:39.364
There was one pulled out.


00:40:39.388 --> 00:40:42.333
So he had me pull my car
in that spot,


00:40:42.358 --> 00:40:45.350
and that way, nobody could
see my car from the road.


00:40:49.199 --> 00:40:53.193
He had me for a couple of hours.


00:40:57.007 --> 00:41:01.155
After he was done
doing what he was doing,


00:41:01.179 --> 00:41:04.390
he threatened me,
took my license.


00:41:04.414 --> 00:41:06.425
He said he would come back
and kill me and my family


00:41:06.449 --> 00:41:10.729
if I reported to the police.


00:41:10.754 --> 00:41:12.766
They did a rape kit on me.


00:41:12.790 --> 00:41:15.735
And then
after they did all that,


00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:18.272
then I went down to the police
station to make a statement.


00:41:18.296 --> 00:41:24.235
The police didn't contact me
again for a long time.


00:41:32.411 --> 00:41:35.757
Amberly was assaulted in 1998.


00:41:35.781 --> 00:41:40.428
The lab for the state of Ohio
finally received enough money


00:41:40.452 --> 00:41:43.765
to hire someone
to put in the DNA


00:41:43.790 --> 00:41:46.901
from all the backlog rape kits
across the state.


00:41:46.925 --> 00:41:49.705
In 2001,that's
when we finally got the call


00:41:49.729 --> 00:41:51.873
that the suspect's DNA


00:41:51.898 --> 00:41:55.243
had been in the computer
and it finally hit.


00:41:55.268 --> 00:41:59.381
So I started looking at why,
why was he in CODIS?


00:41:59.405 --> 00:42:03.785
I read his record. It said
that he had been charged


00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:06.780
with a felony sex offense
against his own wife.


00:42:08.816 --> 00:42:11.860
He raped his wife
in the same manner


00:42:11.884 --> 00:42:13.717
that he raped my victim,
Amberly.


00:42:16.256 --> 00:42:20.002
A Fairfield detective
left her card in our door,


00:42:20.026 --> 00:42:22.996
and said please contact her.


00:42:24.464 --> 00:42:26.343
It was heartbreaking.


00:42:26.367 --> 00:42:28.444
She's a beautiful young girl,


00:42:28.468 --> 00:42:32.382
and she was struggling
really, really hard.


00:42:32.406 --> 00:42:36.451
And... to the point that she
had turned her life to drugs.


00:42:36.476 --> 00:42:41.358
When traumatic experiences
like that happen to you,


00:42:41.382 --> 00:42:43.393
everybody deals with everything
in a different way,


00:42:43.417 --> 00:42:49.032
and it did have something to
do with my addiction issues.


00:42:50.926 --> 00:42:52.769
I made contact with her


00:42:52.793 --> 00:42:55.705
after I got a lineup together.


00:42:55.729 --> 00:42:58.909
When I showed her the lineup, she
couldn't pick Charles Courtney out.


00:42:58.934 --> 00:43:01.311
I could have handed her a
hundred lineups with him in it.


00:43:01.335 --> 00:43:04.848
It wouldn't have made a
difference. She did not recognize him.


00:43:04.873 --> 00:43:06.417
And she told me, she said,


00:43:06.441 --> 00:43:09.386
"Hey, you know,
I didn't look at his face."


00:43:09.411 --> 00:43:11.222
I concentrated
on one of his tattoos


00:43:11.246 --> 00:43:17.029
<i>more than anything else
with him, and, um...</i>


00:43:18.453 --> 00:43:20.932
It was a lion
on his forearm roaring,


00:43:20.957 --> 00:43:23.268
and in the background,
there was a sun.


00:43:23.292 --> 00:43:26.404
You could see the sun
through the lion's mouth roaring.


00:43:26.429 --> 00:43:28.907
Sol took a picture
of every tattoo he had,


00:43:28.931 --> 00:43:30.775
everyone of them.


00:43:30.800 --> 00:43:33.947
Prison tattoo
after prison tattoo.


00:43:33.971 --> 00:43:36.381
And I went back to Amberly,


00:43:36.405 --> 00:43:40.986
and then I started showing her
pictures of each and every tattoo,


00:43:41.011 --> 00:43:44.036
And I left
the lion's head for last.


00:43:46.483 --> 00:43:48.828
As soon as
she showed me that tattoo,


00:43:48.852 --> 00:43:50.377
I knew that was it.


00:43:52.489 --> 00:43:54.899
I knew I had
enough to arrest him.


00:43:54.924 --> 00:43:56.736
<i>So the next day, went up there,</i>


00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:59.772
I called for Hamilton
police officers to back me up.


00:43:59.796 --> 00:44:03.409
Two of them came out,
knocked on the front door,


00:44:03.433 --> 00:44:04.867
and he answered the door.


00:44:06.269 --> 00:44:08.013
He answered the door.


00:44:08.038 --> 00:44:10.883
And I asked him,
"You Charles Courtney?"


00:44:10.907 --> 00:44:14.036
He said, "Yup." I said,
"You are under arrest."


00:44:19.316 --> 00:44:21.684
I think it was the next day.


00:44:30.762 --> 00:44:33.755
She cried. She cried.


00:44:35.967 --> 00:44:37.935
<i>She cried, and I think...</i>


00:44:42.141 --> 00:44:43.335
<i>some relief.</i>


00:44:45.477 --> 00:44:47.955
'Cause I told her, I said,
"He's not gonna do this again.


00:44:47.980 --> 00:44:50.711
He's not gonna get out,
he's not gonna hunt you down.


00:44:53.052 --> 00:44:54.383
This guy's going away.


00:44:55.988 --> 00:44:57.967
There's nothing anybody
can do about it.


00:44:57.991 --> 00:44:59.802
He's going away."


00:44:59.826 --> 00:45:04.873
I never really thought that
he would ever get caught,


00:45:04.898 --> 00:45:07.777
'cause so much time had went by.


00:45:07.801 --> 00:45:12.014
Shortly after I had put
Charles Courtney in jail,


00:45:12.038 --> 00:45:14.983
Los Angeles County
Sheriff's Department called me


00:45:15.008 --> 00:45:19.422
in reference to a case
that they had with Helena


00:45:19.446 --> 00:45:21.792
that occurred in 1996.


00:45:21.816 --> 00:45:23.794
And they had just received a hit


00:45:23.818 --> 00:45:27.764
that Charles Courtney was
also their perpetrator.


00:45:27.788 --> 00:45:30.433
I faxed them everything I had


00:45:30.458 --> 00:45:35.404
and thought that they were going to go
ahead and proceed with Helena's case.


00:45:35.428 --> 00:45:37.773
But there was nothing.


00:45:37.797 --> 00:45:40.143
I didn't hear anything back


00:45:40.167 --> 00:45:42.978
until about 2011.


00:45:43.002 --> 00:45:48.451
From what I understand, Helena
was still pushing for some resolve on it.


00:45:48.476 --> 00:45:51.087
A decade, a decade on a case


00:45:51.111 --> 00:45:54.958
that could have been
resolved for her.


00:45:54.982 --> 00:46:00.029
A decade that
a police department wasted


00:46:00.054 --> 00:46:02.318
and not taking care
of its business.


00:46:11.900 --> 00:46:15.012
I have heard
that the prosecution numbers


00:46:15.036 --> 00:46:18.315
in Los Angeles
are exceedingly low.


00:46:18.340 --> 00:46:22.321
And it's very disturbing to me,
very disturbing.


00:46:22.345 --> 00:46:24.423
I cannot imagine why.


00:46:24.447 --> 00:46:28.326
Because they don't
prosecute cases... rape cases.


00:46:28.351 --> 00:46:31.810
I mean, that's like... that's
like the unspoken problem.


00:46:33.855 --> 00:46:37.903
That even the cases that get
taken to them, they get rejected,


00:46:37.927 --> 00:46:40.105
a huge majority of cases.


00:46:45.168 --> 00:46:47.847
Oh, I'm not aware of that data.


00:46:47.871 --> 00:46:50.049
You're going to have to show
that data to me or share with me,


00:46:50.073 --> 00:46:51.983
so I can actually have it
checked out.


00:46:52.008 --> 00:46:53.089
I'm not aware of that data.


00:46:55.179 --> 00:46:59.826
When we looked
at the 2005 to 2008 cases,


00:46:59.850 --> 00:47:03.396
and we traced them all
the way through the system,


00:47:03.420 --> 00:47:07.934
only 12% of the cases
resulted in a... in arrest.


00:47:07.958 --> 00:47:10.169
I think it was 8% of the cases


00:47:10.194 --> 00:47:12.039
resulted in a conviction,


00:47:12.063 --> 00:47:16.108
and about 5% resulted
in a prison sentence.


00:47:16.133 --> 00:47:21.981
So in other words, one out of
every 20 reports of forcible rape


00:47:22.005 --> 00:47:24.717
reported to the Los Angeles
Police Department


00:47:24.741 --> 00:47:26.886
resulted in a prison sentence.


00:47:38.723 --> 00:47:43.704
When we asked them, you know, what
do you mean by a "righteous victim"?


00:47:43.728 --> 00:47:47.976
They talked about cases
that involve strangers,


00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:52.163
who jump out of the bushes and hold a
gun to her head or a knife to her throat.


00:47:56.208 --> 00:47:58.152
And these are the exception,


00:47:58.177 --> 00:48:00.689
not the rule.


00:48:00.713 --> 00:48:04.993
Cases involving acquaintances
and intimate partners


00:48:05.017 --> 00:48:06.928
are the typical sexual assaults


00:48:06.952 --> 00:48:09.011
that we see reported
to the police.


00:48:11.891 --> 00:48:16.738
The roots are the fact that
this was happening to women,


00:48:16.762 --> 00:48:18.107
for the most part,


00:48:18.131 --> 00:48:22.010
and the misogyny.


00:48:22.035 --> 00:48:24.647
The inequality,


00:48:24.671 --> 00:48:30.020
the scandalous belief that
women are making up these stories,


00:48:30.044 --> 00:48:34.890
all fed into
having a system that was


00:48:34.914 --> 00:48:37.027
more than inefficient,


00:48:37.051 --> 00:48:39.996
it was... it was scandalous.


00:48:40.021 --> 00:48:42.966
That report was
thoroughly discredited.


00:48:42.990 --> 00:48:45.268
I'm aware of that report.


00:48:45.293 --> 00:48:46.803
It was thoroughly discredited.


00:48:46.827 --> 00:48:48.272
It was flawed.


00:48:48.297 --> 00:48:50.741
So I'm not even
going to talk about it.


00:48:50.765 --> 00:48:55.246
I'll refer you to the people who
did the analysis of that report.


00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:56.738
It was thoroughly flawed.


00:48:58.773 --> 00:49:02.786
I did a thorough review
of the research methodology,


00:49:02.810 --> 00:49:04.788
and the research methodology
that was used


00:49:04.812 --> 00:49:06.990
by Spohn and Tellis
is very rigorous.


00:49:07.014 --> 00:49:10.093
From the methodological
research point of view,


00:49:10.117 --> 00:49:14.098
there was nothing that was
problematic with the study.


00:49:14.122 --> 00:49:17.033
When you're going to take
someone's freedom away,


00:49:17.058 --> 00:49:19.804
and impact their lives
and their family's lives,


00:49:19.828 --> 00:49:21.705
by putting them in prison,


00:49:21.730 --> 00:49:25.076
and for sex offenses
in California,


00:49:25.100 --> 00:49:27.945
you go to prison for a
longtime, you better be accurate.


00:49:27.969 --> 00:49:29.847
You better have a high standard.


00:49:29.871 --> 00:49:33.050
And law enforcement sometimes
do bring cases to prosecutors,


00:49:33.075 --> 00:49:36.755
and, ultimately, the decision
is the prosecutor's to make.


00:49:36.779 --> 00:49:41.859
Cooley might find the study
disreputable for a few reasons.


00:49:41.883 --> 00:49:45.063
One is the district attorney
is an elected office,


00:49:45.088 --> 00:49:50.869
and the results suggest that not as many
acquaintance cases were taken forward,


00:49:50.893 --> 00:49:52.327
and that...
That's not good press.


00:50:26.898 --> 00:50:30.844
On average, 86%
of sexual assaults


00:50:30.869 --> 00:50:32.413
that are reported to police


00:50:32.437 --> 00:50:34.381
are never referred
to the prosecutor's office


00:50:34.406 --> 00:50:36.884
even for consideration
of charges.


00:50:36.908 --> 00:50:39.920
The decision that nothing is
going to happen with the case


00:50:39.944 --> 00:50:42.122
happens right there
in the law enforcement agency.


00:50:42.147 --> 00:50:44.291
And when you ask them why
and you look at it,


00:50:44.315 --> 00:50:47.961
a lot of times it goes back
to these types of issues.


00:50:47.986 --> 00:50:49.363
The victim didn't behave right.


00:50:49.387 --> 00:50:53.133
There's something weird
about how she reacted


00:50:53.158 --> 00:50:55.971
that makes us
doubt her creditability.


00:50:55.995 --> 00:50:58.439
We know that between
12% to 50% of rape victims


00:50:58.463 --> 00:51:00.440
experience tonic immobility.


00:51:00.465 --> 00:51:05.046
It is called rape-induced
paralysis in this context,


00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:08.950
where the key word
there is "paralysis."


00:51:08.974 --> 00:51:12.420
The victim cannot move
in the trauma.


00:51:12.445 --> 00:51:16.425
It is hardwired into the body
as a protective mechanism.


00:51:16.449 --> 00:51:19.896
So this is a case study
from a gang rape.


00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:21.898
The victim was frozen,


00:51:21.922 --> 00:51:25.100
as they take turns going in
and sexually assaulting her


00:51:25.124 --> 00:51:27.169
one after another after another.


00:51:27.194 --> 00:51:31.908
Her body did what it needed
to do to try to keep her alive.


00:51:31.932 --> 00:51:36.345
So she goes to the hospital,
she has a rape kit,


00:51:36.369 --> 00:51:40.015
she gives the initial report
to the police officer,


00:51:40.040 --> 00:51:42.952
the police officer refuses
to take the kit.


00:51:42.976 --> 00:51:45.088
He said it was a sloppy mess,


00:51:45.112 --> 00:51:46.723
and he closed the case
right then and there.


00:51:46.747 --> 00:51:48.424
He marked it as "unfounded."


00:51:48.448 --> 00:51:51.361
When I interviewed him later,
I asked him why,


00:51:51.385 --> 00:51:53.230
and here's what he said.


00:51:53.254 --> 00:51:57.000
He said, "She just laid there
so she must have wanted it.


00:51:57.024 --> 00:51:59.002
No one wants to have
a train pulled on them,


00:51:59.026 --> 00:52:00.270
so if she just laid there
and took it,


00:52:00.294 --> 00:52:02.273
she must have wanted it."


00:52:02.297 --> 00:52:04.442
When we look
at how police are trained,


00:52:04.466 --> 00:52:06.143
on the issue of sexual assault,


00:52:06.167 --> 00:52:08.111
more often than not
in the academy,


00:52:08.135 --> 00:52:10.281
what they learn is,
"This is the law,


00:52:10.305 --> 00:52:12.316
these are the different
degrees of the law."


00:52:12.341 --> 00:52:15.453
Do they learn
about victim behavior? No.


00:52:15.477 --> 00:52:17.388
Do they learn
about trauma? No.


00:52:17.412 --> 00:52:20.959
Do they learn about how trauma
impacts memory? No.


00:52:20.984 --> 00:52:24.929
And so they bring to the
interaction an expectation


00:52:24.953 --> 00:52:27.198
that victims should behave
a certain way.


00:52:27.222 --> 00:52:29.400
If you're really traumatized,
you should be crying,


00:52:29.424 --> 00:52:31.401
